## Why This Is Asked
Tests online order-statistics handling with batched updates and amortized reasoning; demonstrates a practical, provable structure.

## Key Concepts
- Order statistics with incremental data
- Amortized analysis over batches
- Two-heap data structure (k smallest vs the rest)
- Space vs time trade-offs

## Code Example
```javascript
class KthSmallest {
  constructor(k) {
    this.k = k;
    this.L = new MaxHeap(); // holds k smallest
    this.R = new MinHeap(); // holds the rest
  }
  insertBatch(arr) {
    for (const x of arr) this.R.push(x);
    while (this.L.size() < this.k && !this.R.empty()) this.L.push(this.R.pop());
    while (!this.R.empty() && this.L.top > this.R.top) {
      const a = this.L.pop();
      const b = this.R.pop();
      this.L.push(b);
      this.R.push(a);
    }
  }
  kth() { return this.L.top; }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How handle duplicates or dynamic k?  
- What are alternatives (order-statistics tree, Fenwick) and trade-offs?  
- How does complexity change if B ≫ k?

## Why This Is Asked\n\nTests understanding of streaming frequency estimation under tight memory. The MG approach gives a bounded-memory alternative to exact counts, with concrete guarantees and practical trade-offs in real-time systems.\n\n## Key Concepts\n- Streaming algorithms, Misra-Gries, bounded counters, frequency estimation, error bounds, exact vs. approximate modes.\n- Trade-offs: k vs accuracy, batch size B vs throughput.\n\n## Code Example\n```javascript
class MGMode {
  constructor(k){
    this.k = k;
    this.counters = new Map();
  }
  add(x){
    if (this.counters.has(x)) this.counters.set(x, this.counters.get(x)+1);
    else if (this.counters.size < this.k) this.counters.set(x,1);
    else {
      for (const key of Array.from(this.counters.keys()))
        this.counters.set(key, this.counters.get(key)-1);
      for (const [key,c] of Array.from(this.counters))
        if (c<=0) this.counters.delete(key);
    }
  }
  estimate(){
    let best = null, bestc = -1;
    for (const [k,c] of this.counters) if (c>bestc){ bestc=c; best=k; }
    return best;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions\n- How would you adapt to a sliding window instead of a growing stream?\n- How to pick k in practice given M and observed N?\n- What are failure modes if the top-1 item rarely exceeds N/(k+1)?

## Why This Is Asked

Demonstrates designing an online, batched-update problem with a practical data structure for order statistics.

## Key Concepts

- Order statistics trees (balanced BST with subtree sizes)
- Batch updates vs. per-element updates
- Memory vs. query speed

## Code Example

```javascript
// Update a batch and fetch median
update(i, newV) {
  const old = a[i];
  tree.decrement(old);
  tree.increment(newV);
  a[i] = newV;
}
median = tree.kth((n+1)//2);
